Come gather round the TV fireplace, kids

Cocoa, caroling, your brother-in-law hiding the Elf on the Shelf in some BS=
 place no one will ever find=E2=80=A6 We all have our Christmas traditions.

And, for many, those include a TV fireplace crackling in the background.

Popular on streaming platforms, TV fireplaces capture some of a real fire's=
 coziness, minus the smoke, cleanup, and risk of burning your house down.

A merry tradition

New York City TV station WPIX ran (http://www.theyulelog.com/htmls/faqs.htm=
l?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-807p3Lo9T_0qad4ACmhMPlv0LljifMsXgGkJdUyxtpXXOxBTpOTVcMhU=
G7fzrzfMJJH8K7-GkvhaXzVO5YD-57R2cyeA&_hsmi=3D392688895 ) the first televise=
d fireplace in 1966 =E2=80=94 =E2=80=9CThe Yule Log=E2=80=9D =E2=80=94 offe=
ring three uninterrupted hours of looped footage of a log burning on Christ=
mas.

Silly and celebrated, a tradition was born.

- Initially, the program was cancelled in 1990 over financial concerns.

- But after years of public pressure, the channel rebooted it in 2001, and =
has aired it every Christmas since.

- In 2011, a similar series, "Fireplace for Your Home," directed by George =
Ford, debuted (https://www.netflix.com/tudum/articles/fireplace-for-your-ho=
me-how-to-watch-the-yule-log-video?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-807p3Lo9T_0qad4ACmhMPlv=
0LljifMsXgGkJdUyxtpXXOxBTpOTVcMhUG7fzrzfMJJH8K7-GkvhaXzVO5YD-57R2cyeA&_hsmi=
=3D392688895 ) on Netflix.

Despite initial skepticism, Ford's videos became some of the platform's mos=
t unlikely hits =E2=80=94 and one of the streaming industry's quietest mark=
eting hacks.

Isn't it ironic?

The original =E2=80=9CYule Log=E2=80=9D program is essentially anti-commerc=
ial. It's an uninterrupted, unprofitable gift to New Yorkers =E2=80=94 but =
its modern-day descendants are some of streaming's most versatile commercia=
l assets.

TV fireplaces tap into holiday nostalgia that can generate serious engageme=
